# CODEOWNERS assigns reviewers to the trust-critical paths below. Assignment
# alone does not block a merge: it becomes an enforced boundary only when the
# branch ruleset requires code-owner review. As of this writing the ruleset
# requires status checks but NOT code-owner review, so this file documents the
# intended review ownership rather than a gate that is currently enforced.
# Enforcing it needs a second trusted reviewer: GitHub does not let a PR author
# approve their own change. Until one is added, `trust-base` is the automated
# boundary and this file makes the remaining human-review requirement explicit.
#
# Why this matters: the automatic CI check (safeinstall-trust.yml) catches
# inconsistent tampering, but a fully consistent rewrite of the whole baseline
# in one PR is only caught by a human reviewing these diffs — which is exactly
# the review this file is meant to gate once the ruleset enforces it.
#
# Order matters: the last matching pattern wins, so the default comes first
# and the trust-critical paths below stay explicit even if the default ever
# changes.

# Default: everything needs review.
*                                     @Mickdownunder

# Trust surface baseline and policy config (the files the lock protects).
/.safeinstall/                        @Mickdownunder
/safeinstall.config.json              @Mickdownunder
/.claude/settings.json                @Mickdownunder
/.cursor/hooks.json                   @Mickdownunder

# CI re-verification anchor and this review map itself.
/.github/workflows/                   @Mickdownunder
/.github/CODEOWNERS                   @Mickdownunder

# Trust-surface and guard source: the code that computes the baseline,
# verifies the ledger, scaffolds CI, and enforces the guard hooks.
/src/trust-surface.ts                 @Mickdownunder
/src/trust-flow.ts                    @Mickdownunder
/src/trust-ledger.ts                  @Mickdownunder
/src/trust-ci.ts                      @Mickdownunder
/src/guard-flow.ts                    @Mickdownunder
/src/guard-setup.ts                   @Mickdownunder
/src/guard-commands.ts                @Mickdownunder
